Hadoop Single-Node Cluster and WordCount
Cloud Computing Lab Exam Notes

Covers questions 45–48: single-node Hadoop setup, pseudo-distributed services and HDFS commands, MapReduce WordCount, and HDFS file upload/list/retrieval.

1. Install Java and Hadoop prerequisites (Q45)

Run commands in an Ubuntu/Linux terminal. Hadoop version and Java compatibility can vary by lab; use the versions specified by your instructor.

sudo apt update
sudo apt install openjdk-11-jdk ssh rsync -y
java -version

Download and extract the Hadoop binary distribution provided/approved for your lab. Example for Hadoop 3.3.6:

cd ~
wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/hadoop/common/hadoop-3.3.6/hadoop-3.3.6.tar.gz
tar -xzf hadoop-3.3.6.tar.gz
mv hadoop-3.3.6 hadoop

2. Set environment variables (Q45)

cat >> ~/.bashrc <<'EOF'
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64
export HADOOP_HOME=$HOME/hadoop
export HADOOP_INSTALL=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_MAPRED_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_COMMON_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_HDFS_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export YARN_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export PATH=$PATH:$HADOOP_HOME/bin:$HADOOP_HOME/sbin
EOF

source ~/.bashrc

If your Java path differs, find it with: readlink -f $(which java)

3. Configure Hadoop files (Q45)

Edit each file under $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/.

hadoop-env.sh

# Ensure this line is present:
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64

core-site.xml

<configuration>
  <property>
    <name>fs.defaultFS</name>
    <value>hdfs://localhost:9000</value>
  </property>
</configuration>

hdfs-site.xml

<configuration>
  <property>
    <name>dfs.replication</name>
    <value>1</value>
  </property>
  <property>
    <name>dfs.namenode.name.dir</name>
    <value>file:///home/YOUR_USERNAME/hadoopdata/namenode</value>
  </property>
  <property>
    <name>dfs.datanode.data.dir</name>
    <value>file:///home/YOUR_USERNAME/hadoopdata/datanode</value>
  </property>
</configuration>

mapred-site.xml

<configuration>
  <property>
    <name>mapreduce.framework.name</name>
    <value>yarn</value>
  </property>
</configuration>

yarn-site.xml

<configuration>
  <property>
    <name>yarn.nodemanager.aux-services</name>
    <value>mapreduce_shuffle</value>
  </property>
</configuration>

Replace YOUR_USERNAME with the output of whoami, or use a correct absolute home directory. Create the NameNode/DataNode directories before formatting.

4. Configure passwordless SSH and start Hadoop (Q46)

sudo service ssh start
ssh-keygen -t rsa -P '' -f ~/.ssh/id_rsa
cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
ssh localhost

# Format the NameNode only on first setup:
hdfs namenode -format

# Start HDFS and YARN:
start-dfs.sh
start-yarn.sh

# Check running Java services:
jps

Do not repeatedly format the NameNode after storing HDFS data; formatting can make existing HDFS metadata/data inaccessible. In a VM/container where systemd is unavailable, use the supported SSH service command for that environment.

5. HDFS commands (Q46 and Q48)

hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /user/$USER/input
echo "Hadoop is a distributed computing framework. Hadoop processes big data." > sample.txt

# Upload local file to HDFS
hdfs dfs -put -f sample.txt /user/$USER/input/

# List files
hdfs dfs -ls /user/$USER/input

# Display file contents
hdfs dfs -cat /user/$USER/input/sample.txt

# Download file from HDFS to local directory
hdfs dfs -get /user/$USER/input/sample.txt ./downloaded.txt
cat downloaded.txt

6. Run MapReduce WordCount (Q47)

Create input text and upload it to HDFS:

mkdir -p ~/wordcount-input
echo "hello hadoop hello cloud" > ~/wordcount-input/input.txt
echo "hadoop cloud computing" >> ~/wordcount-input/input.txt

hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /wordcount/input
hdfs dfs -put -f ~/wordcount-input/input.txt /wordcount/input/

# Remove old output directory if it exists
hdfs dfs -rm -r -f /wordcount/output

# Run the built-in WordCount example JAR
hadoop jar $HADOOP_HOME/share/hadoop/mapreduce/hadoop-mapreduce-examples-*.jar wordcount /wordcount/input /wordcount/output

# Display word-frequency output
hdfs dfs -cat /wordcount/output/part-r-00000

Expected counts for the sample input (case-sensitive):

cloud  2\ncomputing  1\nhadoop  2\nhello  2

7. Useful checks and troubleshooting

Check Java: java -version

Check Hadoop: hadoop version

Check daemons: jps (look for NameNode, DataNode, ResourceManager and NodeManager)

Check HDFS health/listing: hdfs dfs -ls /

If SSH fails, verify SSH is running and passwordless localhost login works.

If a job fails because output exists, remove its HDFS output directory before rerunning.

If Hadoop reports JAVA_HOME errors, correct JAVA_HOME in both ~/.bashrc and hadoop-env.sh.

If the example JAR wildcard does not resolve as expected, list the files in $HADOOP_HOME/share/hadoop/mapreduce/.

